title: Thrombosis in PNH
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Pattern:'' Predominantly venous; arterial events also occur.
** Hepatic veins; portal and mesenteric veins.
** Cerebral veins and dural sinuses.
** Conventional deep vein thrombosis and pulmonary embolism also occur.
* ''Budd–Chiari syndrome:'' Hepatic vein thrombosis.
** Classic presentation: abdominal pain, hepatomegaly, ascites.
** New abdominal pain warrants considering abdominal venous thrombosis.
** Do not automatically attribute pain to hemolysis.
* ''Mechanisms:'' Multifactorial, incompletely understood; proposed contributors include:
** Complement-mediated platelet activation.
** Intravascular hemolysis releases free hemoglobin, scavenging nitric oxide.
*** Nitric oxide depletion promotes platelet activation and endothelial dysfunction.
** Hemolysis alone does not explain thrombosis.
* ''Diagnosis:'' Unexplained unusual-site thrombosis should prompt PNH testing.
** Especially with cytopenias or direct antiglobulin test–negative hemolysis.
** Test using peripheral-blood flow cytometry.
* ''Acute treatment:'' Prompt therapeutic anticoagulation unless contraindicated.
** Initiate or optimize complement inhibition.
** See [[Eculizumab and ravulizumab in PNH]].
** Anticoagulation alone does not control complement-driven risk.
* ''Anticoagulant duration:'' Individualize with specialist input.
** Consider bleeding risk, clot resolution, sustained disease control.
** Stopping after effective complement blockade has limited evidence.

Sources: ASH SAP, 9th ed., ch. 14, pp. 166–167; [[Hill 2013|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23610373/]]; [[Brodsky 2021|https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33512400/]]; [[Budd–Chiari review 2015|https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4669515/]].
